<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Clovis I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 466–511 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> · Also known as Clovis · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merovingian-dynasty">Merovingian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Clovis I was king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> in the late fifth and early sixth centuries and founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merovingian-dynasty">Merovingian dynasty</a>. He united most of the Frankish tribes, conquered much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and converted to Catholic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. His kingdom was the foundation on which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a> was later built.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/clovis-i.jpg" alt="Clovis I" width="185" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pethrus · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Remy_baptise_Clovis_d%C3%A9tail.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Gregory of Tours speaks of a single Alemannic king killed in battle with <b>Clovis</b>, which may indicate greater unity by the late 5th century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alemanni">Alemanni · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Status rested on kinship, land, cattle and service in the war bands of kings and nobles, and later Frankish law, the Pactus Legis Salicae issued under <b>Clovis I</b>, reflects this society in its tariffs of compensation, or wergild, for the killing of free Franks, Romans and the half-free.</blockquote><a href="/empire/franks">Franks · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>His son <b>Clovis I</b> succeeded him, traditionally in 481.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-franks">Kingdom of the Franks · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Franks under <b>Clovis I</b> ended this expansion: in 507, allied with the Burgundians, they killed Alaric II at the Battle of Campus Vogladensis near Poitiers and sacked Toulouse.</blockquote><a href="/empire/visigothic-kingdom">Visigothic Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the West, missionaries converted the Germanic, Celtic and later Scandinavian and Slavic peoples between the 5th and 11th centuries; <b>Clovis</b>, king of the Franks, was baptised around 500, and the Rus of Kyiv adopted Orthodox Christianity under Prince Volodymyr in 988.</blockquote><a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Childebert died after a long illness without heirs, allowing his brother Chlothar I to reunite the Frankish kingdom, as their father <b>Clovis I</b> had done, and to seize Childebert's treasure.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-23">23 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=495" aria-label="Paris on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4604 458 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Clovis I"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5054 728h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=495">What was here in 495 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;For I have invoked my own gods, but, as I find, they have withdrawn from aiding me; and therefore I believe that they possess no power, since they do not help those who obey them.&#8221;</p><footer>Prayer in the battle against the Alamanni (Gregory of Tours, History of the Franks, book 2) &#183; tr. Ernest Brehaut, 1916 &#183; Gregory of Tours' account, written about eighty years later &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/74955/pg74955-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I now call upon thee, I desire to believe thee, only let me be rescued from my adversaries.&#8221;</p><footer>Prayer in the battle against the Alamanni (Gregory of Tours, History of the Franks, book 2) &#183; tr. Ernest Brehaut, 1916 &#183; Gregory of Tours' account, written about eighty years later &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/74955/pg74955-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
